sections:
  - eyebrow: "The problem"
    h: "A sinkhole in a public road is, above all, a question of cause"
    html: "A sinkhole opening in an urban road immediately raises a question of public liability: there is damage to public land, a risk to people and vehicles, and several parties with opposing interests —the highway authority, the utility concessionaire and their public-liability insurers—. Before deciding who bears the cost, one technical question has to be answered: <b>why did the ground collapse?</b><br><br>The forensic engineer's task is precisely to order that answer methodically, so that the later argument over responsibility rests on a diagnosis rather than a hypothesis."
  - eyebrow: "The instruction"
    h: "Make safe first, investigate second"
    html: "The first measure was not to investigate but to <b>contain</b>: securing the perimeter and confirming that the failure did not extend beyond the visible cavity, ruling out a larger collapse in progress.<br><br>With the situation stabilised, the <b>ground investigation</b> was defined and coordinated: a detailed geological inspection and two <b>super-heavy dynamic probing (DPSH)</b> tests to establish the thickness of the near-surface made ground and the depth of the underlying stratum. The field investigation was carried out by a specialist geological practice <b>under forensic coordination</b>; the expert's role is to direct the investigation, integrate its results and translate them into a diagnosis of cause —not to carry out the tests."
  - eyebrow: "The findings"
    h: "A local ground model of the collapse point"
    html: "The investigation allowed a <b>local ground model</b> to be built, with distinct geotechnical units: a near-surface layer of pavement and formation over compacted fill and, beneath it, the natural stratum. The DPSH tests bounded the thickness of the surface made ground and the depth at which competent ground appears.<br><br>One result was decisive in closing off hypotheses: at the depth relevant to the collapse, <b>a local aquifer was ruled out</b>, which steers the search for the cause towards the behaviour of the fill and the buried services, and away from a natural groundwater dynamic."
  - eyebrow: "The analysis"
    h: "Why the cause determines the responsibility"
    html: "In a sinkhole, the cause is not an academic detail: it is what decides who is liable. The usual hypotheses lead to different responsible parties.<ul style=\"margin:18px 0 0; padding-left:20px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;\"><li><b>A leak in a buried service.</b> Pressurised water washes out and carries away fines from the fill until a cavity forms: attention turns to the utility concessionaire and the condition of its network.</li><li><b>Poorly compacted fill.</b> An embankment placed below the required density settles and collapses over time: responsibility points to the construction and its quality control.</li><li><b>Internal erosion of the ground.</b> The progressive washing-out of particles by water flow, which requires the natural mechanism to be distinguished from one induced by a leak.</li></ul><br>The forensic action orders that decision chain —<b>make safe → investigate → diagnose → attribute</b>— so that each link rests on the one before it."
  - eyebrow: "The outcome"
    h: "A technical basis for allocating responsibility"
    html: "The file gives the owner and its insurers a ground model at the collapse point and a diagnosis of cause supported by objective investigation, not conjecture. On that basis, responsibility can be argued between the authority, the concessionaire and the public-liability insurers, and the remediation can be sized with judgement."
  - eyebrow: "Who it serves"
    h: "Authorities, concessionaires and insurers"
    html: "This kind of work is aimed at <b>local authorities and mixed service-management companies</b>, water, drainage and highway <b>concessionaires</b>, and <b>public-liability insurers</b>. In every case the value is the same: turning an incident on the public highway into a technically bounded problem, with a diagnosed cause and a defensible chain of responsibility."
